  "strategy": "1. **Approach and rationale**:\nWe will use a **probabilistic self-assembly** approach to construct the function. Instead of direct numerical optimization, we interpret the function as a physical system that emerges from a probability distribution. This leverages the intuition that minimization of overlap corresponds to unsupervised formation of patterns, similar to crystal structure optimization.\n\nThe core idea is to:\n- Start with random bits (0/1 values) on the domain [0,2] with exactly N/2 ones\n- Iteratively learn distributions that minimize the maximum cross-entropy penalty\n- Use a physics-inspired annealing process with adaptive temperature\n- Track the most-stable patterns through renormalization\n\nThis differs from previous strategies in that it:\n- Treats the function as an emergent pattern rather than a design variable\n- Uses continuous probability distributions\n- Incorporates concepts from statistical physics\n- Leverages renormalization principles\n\nLimitations include:\n- Exports an approximate solution\n- Requires careful tuning of learning rates\n- Theoretical guarantees are limited by the stochastic nature\n\n2. **Mathematicalspecification**:\nRepresent h(x) as:\n    h(x) = \u03c3(w(x))\n    w(x) = \u03b8(x) \u2299 p(x)\n\nWhere:\n- \u03c3 is sigmoid activation\n- \u03b8(x) \u2208 {-1, 1}\n- p(x) is piecewise constant\n- \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n\nObjective:\nmin h(x) \u21d2 max_k \u222b\u2096 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nWith renormalization:\nL2(\u03b8(x)) + \u03b1 \u064a\u0646\u0628\u063a\u064a\u00d7KL(p(x) || q(data))\n\n3. **Implementationprocedure**:\n6. **Parent Handling**:\n    - Initialize with `initial_h_values`\n    - Project to probability form while maintaining integral constraint\n7. **Core Optimization**:\n    - **Learning phase** (2\u00d7 y budget):\n        Increase temperature exponentially\n        Update \u03b8(x) using Boltzmann distribution\n        Adjust p(x) with continuous bandit algorithm\n        Track gradient innovations\n    - **Annealing phase** (remaining budget):\n        Coarsen grid via renormalization\n        Use fast adaptive mode-seeking (SGLD)\n        Downsample while preserving structure\n8. **Termination**:\n    - Return best solution found\n    - Include probability-based validity check\n    - Automatically handle grid reduction\n\n4. **Searchchoicesandvariations**:\n- **Base config**: Start temp=1.0, learning rate=0.5\n- **Cooling schedule**: Exponential decay with base=0.95 every 500 steps\n- **Mode searching**:\n    - Use sparse SVT for matrix factorization guidance\n    - When stuck, perform grid doubling (up to n=2000)\n- **Contingency**:\n    - If no improvement, reset properly\n    - Each failure triggers different algorithm\n    - Progressive complexity:\n\n5. **Computebudgetandrobustness**:\nDominant costs:\n- Learning phase: O(n\u00d7log(time))\n- Renormalization: O(n_min\u2074average)\n- Mode seeking: O(number_modes\u00d7n)\nFeasible n_paths:\n- CUDA GPUs if available\n- Hybrid pool parallelism\n- 90% completion within first hour\n\n6. **Validationandreturncontract**:\nValidate via:\n    - Integral: \u2211h[i]\u00d7dx \u2248 1\n    - Bounds: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n    - Cross-entropy computation\nFall back to simulation-based verification",
